{"notes":[{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["selective generation","functionality alignment","code generation"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\njeong2026towards,\ntitle={Towards Functional Correctness of Code Models with Selective Generation},\nauthor={Jaewoo Jeong and Taesoo Kim and Sangdon Park},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=1CQZbl24wp}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Towards Functional Correctness of Code Models with Selective Generation"},"paperhash":{"value":"jeong|towards_functional_correctness_of_code_models_with_selective_generation"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/31d6c8dcf65880a30a6e6455994fecb8ffcd05d3.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a learning algorithm for selective code generation that controls the rate of hallucination by exploiting a code analysis method, called fuzzing, to generate unit tests for a code correctness measure."},"primary_area":{"value":"social_aspects->safety"},"abstract":{"value":"The hallucination of code generation models hinders their applicability to systems requiring higher safety standards. One critical bottleneck in addressing code hallucination is the difficulty of identifying the functional correctness of generated code, due to its unnatural form. We address this core bottleneck by automatically generating unit tests using dynamic code analysis tools, leveraging the executable nature of code. Accordingly, we propose a selective code generator that abstains from uncertain generations -- based on the functional correctness evaluated by generated unit tests -- to theoretically control the correctness among non-abstained answers, i.e., the false discovery rate. Finally, we propose to use generated unit tests in evaluation as well as in learning for precise code evaluation, calling this paradigm FuzzEval. We demonstrate the efficacy of our method along with the controllability of code hallucination and reasonable selection efficiency."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/eafbcf98758c8a271344e47d8df50c8702364fc1.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Code hallucinations hinders the practical use of large language models to software engineering, but difficult to address due to un-natural form of code. We tackle this challenge through selective generation, where the model abstains from answering if the model determines that the generated code is likely to be incorrect.\n\nTo determine whether the generated code is correct during calibration stage, we automatically generate and execute unit tests using techniques such as fuzzing. Based on these tests, we propose a method that is theoretically guaranteed and validated under diverse experimental setups. \n\nOur findings enables the reliable deployment of LLM-generated code by reducing potentially incorrect code."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jaewoo_Jeong2","~Taesoo_Kim1","~Sangdon_Park1"]},"authors":{"value":["Jaewoo Jeong","Taesoo Kim","Sangdon Park"]}},"tmdate":1790067394930,"pdate":1777576624782,"tcdate":1769149640144,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission21401/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission21401/Authors"],"forum":"1CQZbl24wp","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":21401,"cdate":1769149640144,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission21401/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission21401/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067394930,"odate":1782341982162,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"1CQZbl24wp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a learning algorithm for selective code generation that controls the rate of hallucination by exploiting a code analysis method, called fuzzing, to generate unit tests for a code correctness measure."},"keywords":{"value":["selective generation","dynamic code analysis","code generation"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"The hallucination of code generation models hinders their applicability to systems requiring higher safety standards. One critical bottleneck in addressing code hallucination is the difficulty of identifying the functional correctness of generated code, due to its unnatural form. We address this core bottleneck by automatically generating unit tests using dynamic code analysis tools, leveraging the \\emph{executable nature} of code. Accordingly, we propose \\emph{selective code generator} that abstains from uncertain generations -- based on the functional correctness evaluated by generated unit tests -- to {\\color{red}theoretically control the correctness among non-abstained answers, \\ie the false discovery rate}. Finally, we propose to use generated unit tests in evaluation as well as in learning for precise code evaluation, calling this paradigm \\emph{FuzzEval}. We demonstrate the efficacy of our method along with the controllability of code hallucination and reasonable selection efficiency."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\njeong2026ensuring,\ntitle={Ensuring Functional Correctness of Large Code Models with Selective Generation},\nauthor={Jaewoo Jeong and Taesoo Kim and Sangdon Park},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=0pRvnSBNGx}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Ensuring Functional Correctness of Large Code Models with Selective Generation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ed8320ac89e22df30c55e73de92648870789a235.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"jeong|ensuring_functional_correctness_of_large_code_models_with_selective_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jaewoo_Jeong2","~Taesoo_Kim1","~Sangdon_Park1"]},"authors":{"value":["Jaewoo Jeong","Taesoo Kim","Sangdon Park"]}},"tmdate":1770805023406,"tcdate":1758244406762,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14828/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14828/Authors"],"forum":"0pRvnSBNGx","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":14828,"cdate":1758244406762,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14828/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770805023406,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"0pRvnSBNGx","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a learning algorithm for selective code generation that controls the rate of hallucination by exploiting a code analysis method, called fuzzing, to generate unit tests for a code correctness measure."},"venue":{"value":"DL4C @ NeurIPS 2025 Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f30973b23de9feaecd7ac724420a1bd374b2c574.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["code generation","selective generation","dynamic code analysis"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C"},"paperhash":{"value":"jeong|ensuring_functional_correctness_of_large_code_models_with_selective_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jaewoo_Jeong2","~Taesoo_Kim1","~Sangdon_Park1"]},"abstract":{"value":"The hallucination of code generation models hinders their applicability to systems requiring higher safety standards. One critical bottleneck in addressing code hallucination is the difficulty of identifying the functional correctness of generated code, due to its unnatural form. We address this core bottleneck by automatically generating unit tests using dynamic code analysis tools, leveraging the \\emph{executable nature} of code. Accordingly, we propose \\emph{selective code generator} that abstains from uncertain generations -- based on the functional correctness evaluated by generated unit tests -- to theoretically control the correctness among non-abstained answers, \\ie the false discovery rate. Finally, we propose to use generated unit tests in evaluation as well as in learning for precise code evaluation, calling this paradigm \\emph{FuzzEval}. We demonstrate the efficacy of our method along with the controllability of code hallucination and reasonable selection efficiency."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\njeong2025ensuring,\ntitle={Ensuring Functional Correctness of Large Code Models with Selective Generation},\nauthor={Jaewoo Jeong and Taesoo Kim and Sangdon Park},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2025 Fourth Workshop on Deep Learning for Code},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=qzAnF8x0GE}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Ensuring Functional Correctness of Large Code Models with Selective Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Jaewoo Jeong","Taesoo Kim","Sangdon Park"]}},"tmdate":1764073174209,"pdate":1758551724738,"tcdate":1756294818023,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission39/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission39/Authors"],"forum":"qzAnF8x0GE","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":39,"cdate":1756294818023,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission39/-/Revision"],"mdate":1764073174209,"odate":1764073174192,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C","id":"qzAnF8x0GE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 Workshop DL4C Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/96d116a02dc1e8ed5f45ed9f6a8d165b639fa6e9.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["selective generation","conformal risk control","code security","abstention","static analysis","equivalence classes"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C"},"paperhash":{"value":"rushdi|selective_code_generation_under_correctness_and_security_risk"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ahmad_A_Rushdi1"]},"abstract":{"value":"LLM-generated code can pass every unit test and still contain known-vulnerable patterns. Existing selective-generation methods --- pass@$1$, best-of-$K$ on tests, and one-objective conformal abstention --- accept it silently. We extend Conformal Risk Control (CRC) to the joint event of accepting code that fails a test verifier or triggers a static analyzer. Our wrapper, \\emph{joint-CRC}, samples $K$ candidates per problem, clusters by joint test-pass / static-analysis-clean behavior, and accepts when the largest good cluster clears a calibrated threshold; one CRC call bounds the joint silent-failure rate at a user-chosen $\\alpha$. On thirteen function-level CWE prompts ($K{=}8$, two generators), joint-CRC accepts correct-and-clean code in $82.6\\pm1.4\\%$ of cases with no observed joint silent failures, while pass@$1$ and pass@$1$+Semgrep gate leak joint-bad code at $42\\%$ and $26\\%$. A security-only CRC baseline achieves zero security silent failures yet still leaks $34\\%$ joint-bad code, directly showing that controlling either axis alone does not control the joint event. Our experiments demonstrate calibrated control of the verifier-defined joint event; controlling hidden correctness failures beyond the supplied tests requires a held-out test split, left to future work."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nrushdi2026selective,\ntitle={Selective Code Generation under Correctness and Security Risk},\nauthor={Ahmad A Rushdi},\nbooktitle={Deep Learning for Code: Towards Human-Centered Coding Agents},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=iTWFQ8bnkP}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Selective Code Generation under Correctness and Security Risk"},"authors":{"value":["Ahmad A Rushdi"]}},"tmdate":1782424883059,"pdate":1781618628061,"tcdate":1778900267824,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/Submission107/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/Submission107/Authors"],"forum":"iTWFQ8bnkP","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","number":107,"cdate":1778900267824,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/Submission107/-/Revision"],"mdate":1782424883059,"odate":1781618628061,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C","id":"iTWFQ8bnkP","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"code language models, dpo, sft, language models, optimisation, python"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2024 April Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2024codeoptimise,\ntitle={Code-Optimise: Optimising Code Language Models for Functional Correctness and Efficiency},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - April 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=qwD7a6QgsU},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Code-Optimise: Optimising Code Language Models for Functional Correctness and Efficiency"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment","Approaches low compute settings-efficiency","Publicly available software and/or pre-trained models","Data resources"]},"abstract":{"value":"Code Language Models are capable of generating solutions that are fairly functionally correct and efficient. However, previous work has focused on improving either functional correctness or efficiency, usually at the expense of the other. To this end, we introduce Code-Optimise, a lightweight optimisation for Code Language Models that incorporates learning signals for correctness (pass, fail) as well as code efficiency (fast, slow). During training, Code-Optimise dynamically selects solutions from our self-generated code preference data to reduce overfitting. Code-Optimise achieves significant improvements in $pass@k$ while decreasing average runtime by up to 6\\% for cheaper code execution. It also reduces the average length of generated solutions by up to 23\\% for HumanEval and up to 48\\% for MBPP for faster response / inference times while demonstrating the fastest overall (single) solutions ($best@k$)."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7b8497cd763f685378f78964a872615de2d6660a.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"NLP Applications"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794063139246,"tcdate":1713265190056,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission760/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission760/Authors"],"forum":"qwD7a6QgsU","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":760,"cdate":1713265190056,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission760/-/Blind_Submission_License_Agreement","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794063139246,"odate":1713279540366,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April","id":"qwD7a6QgsU","version":2}],"count":10000}